package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part story: The Lighthouse of Paper Stars. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch58 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_lighthouse_of_paper_stars",
            title = "The Lighthouse of Paper Stars",
            tagline = "A library's annual paper-star display helps a young volunteer uncover the quiet history of a reading room.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Star in the Window", listOf(
                    "Every December, the public library placed paper stars in its tall front window. This year, one star was different: it had a tiny lighthouse drawn in its centre. Nneka, a student volunteer, asked the librarian whether it belonged to a special display. The librarian said it had appeared in the decoration box for years, but its maker was unknown.",
                    "The library was preparing a display about the history of its reading room. Nneka offered to help sort the decorations and found a folded note inside the lighthouse star. It read, 'For the people who helped us find our way.' There was no name or date, so the librarian suggested researching the display's history before assigning a meaning."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Old Photograph", listOf(
                    "The archive contained a photograph of the library window from twenty-five years earlier. The same lighthouse star appeared near the centre, surrounded by paper boats and lanterns. A caption mentioned a winter reading celebration, but it did not identify the decorations' makers. Nneka wondered whether the star had been part of that first event.",
                    "The librarian found an old programme listing a volunteer reading room that had operated during building renovations. Residents had helped keep books available in a temporary space. The lighthouse might have represented the idea of finding a place to read, but the archive did not prove that interpretation."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "A Former Volunteer", listOf(
                    "The library contacted a former volunteer, Mr Ekanem, who had helped with the temporary reading room. He remembered the winter celebration and said the paper decorations had been made by visitors of all ages. He could not identify the lighthouse star, but he remembered that children had been invited to draw symbols of welcome.",
                    "Nneka asked whether the library could record his memories. He agreed to a short interview and reviewed the notes before they were added to the archive. He spoke about the temporary room, the donated books, and the volunteers who took turns keeping the space tidy. He did not want the display to focus on one person."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Reading Room", listOf(
                    "The renovation records showed that the temporary reading room had been set up in a community hall. It stayed open for several months while the library's main floor was repaired. The volunteers arranged book boxes by age range, kept a borrowing notebook, and helped visitors find the right shelf. The lighthouse star had been made during one of the children's craft afternoons, though the maker remained unknown.",
                    "Nneka realised that the star's story was larger than its creator. The librarian suggested a display about shared effort, with a note that the star's individual maker had not been recorded. The students could add a new star with their own message, but they should not pretend to know who made the original."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "A New Craft Afternoon", listOf(
                    "The library organised a paper-star workshop. Visitors could make a star, draw a symbol, or simply write a short message of thanks. Participation was optional, and the librarian explained that finished decorations might be displayed in the public window. Anyone who preferred to keep their work could take it home.",
                    "Nneka helped prepare safe paper materials and simple instructions. A younger visitor drew a book with wings, while another made a bright yellow sun. One child created a lighthouse and said it reminded them of the library sign. The librarian asked whether the child wanted the star displayed, and the child happily agreed."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "The Display Plan", listOf(
                    "The library designed three sections: the temporary reading room, the volunteers who supported it, and the annual paper-star tradition. Each historical statement was checked against the archive or an approved interview. The lighthouse star would be shown with a label explaining what was known and what remained uncertain.",
                    "The librarian reviewed the wording carefully. She wanted visitors to understand that an unknown maker did not make an object unimportant. Nneka added a line inviting people to share memories of the reading room through the library's public contact desk, without asking them to reveal private details."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Window", listOf(
                    "When the display opened, visitors recognised the old community hall in the photographs. Mr Ekanem attended and answered questions about the temporary reading room. He smiled at the original lighthouse star and said he was glad it had survived, even though he could not remember who had folded it.",
                    "A visitor suggested that the lighthouse might have been made by her older sister, but she was not certain. The librarian recorded the suggestion as an unverified memory and did not change the label. Nneka understood that a good archive could welcome possibilities without turning them into facts."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "A Message for Readers", listOf(
                    "The new paper stars were placed around the old one. Each carried a short message chosen by its maker. Some thanked librarians; others celebrated a favourite book or welcomed new readers. The display included a note that the messages were voluntary and that visitors could enjoy the window without contributing anything.",
                    "The library held a reading afternoon in the renovated room. Mr Ekanem read a short public-domain story, and Nneka helped younger visitors choose books. The event connected the old temporary room with the library's present work, showing how shared spaces can continue to welcome people after a building changes."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Star's Keeper", listOf(
                    "After the season, the original lighthouse star was placed in an archival sleeve. The new decorations were returned to their makers or stored with permission. The librarian updated the catalogue with the star's known history, the interview reference, and a note that its maker was still unidentified.",
                    "Nneka helped write a guide for future volunteers about recording donations and asking before displaying artwork. The guide explained that a person's choice could change and that the library should respect requests to remove or return an item. The small star had taught her as much about careful memory as about decoration."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "A Light Left On", listOf(
                    "The following year, the library repeated the paper-star tradition. The original lighthouse star appeared in a small display case, and a new one hung in the window. The label told visitors that the first star came from a community craft afternoon connected to the temporary reading room, while its maker remained unknown.",
                    "Nneka watched a family stop to read the display before entering the library. She thought about the volunteers who had kept books available during renovations and the children who had folded stars without expecting them to last for decades. The lighthouse was only paper, but its message had endured: a welcoming place can help people find their way."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
